## Before you write any DSL: read the local docs

**The Magik DSL is not in your training data.** `model`, `screen`, `action`, `ledger`, `channel`,
`flow`, `component` — none of it. Writing them from memory produces confident, Rails-shaped code
for a framework that does not work that way, and it is the single most expensive mistake available
in this repository.

The gem ships its own documentation. It is on disk, it is instant, and it is **the documentation for
the exact magik version this app has** — which no web page can promise.

```bash
magik docs path                  # the directory. Point your normal grep/glob/read tools at it
magik docs list                  # every page and its slug
magik docs search ledger         # find the page before you guess the API
magik docs Models                # read one page
```

`magik docs path` is the one to reach for first: it turns the shipped docs into ordinary files your
existing search already handles. **Do not web-search the DSL** — it is slow, rate-limited, and
returns whatever is on `main` rather than what your gem does.

`magik docs` works today. Almost nothing else does.

## The framework is not implemented

**`As of <%= generated_on %>` this app cannot run.** Magik `<%= magik_version %>` is a
name-reservation release: `App.define`, `model`, `screen`, `action`, the router and the server are
**specified and not implemented**. `magik server`, `magik test` and `magik check` exit `1` with
`MAGIK_COMMAND_NOT_IMPLEMENTED`.

Check rather than trust this paragraph:

```bash
magik version --json     # {"version":"<%= magik_version %>","status":"spec only",…}
magik help               # every command, each marked `ready` or `planned`
```

You can write declarations, docs and a plan today. You cannot boot, migrate, render or test.
**Never write a comment, commit message or doc claiming otherwise.** This section leaves the
template when Phases 1–2 land; while it is here, it is true.

## Layout — the file tree is part of the product

One declaration per file, and the path says what the file declares before you open it.

| Declaration | Lives in |
|---|---|
| `model :Invoice` | `app/models/invoice.rb` |
| `screen :Invoices` | `app/screens/invoices.rb` |
| `component :MoneyCell` | `app/components/money_cell.rb` |
| `action :mark_paid` | `app/actions/mark_paid.rb` |
| `job :SendInvoiceEmail` | `app/jobs/send_invoice_email.rb` |
| `channel :invoice_updates` | `app/channels/invoice_updates.rb` |
| `ledger :Receivables` | `app/ledgers/receivables.rb` |
| `migrate :CreateInvoices` | `db/migrations/002_create_invoices.rb` — hand-written, append-only |
| any test | `test/<kind>/<name>_test.rb`, mirroring `app/` exactly |

Constants are `snake_case`d for the filename; an `action` name is already a symbol and the filename
is that symbol. A file declaring two things, or the wrong kind for its directory, is refused —
`MAGIK_FILE_MULTIPLE_DECLARATIONS`, `MAGIK_DECLARATION_MISPLACED`.

`config/app.rb` is the composition root: `App.define :<%= app_class %>`. It holds what is true of the
whole app — tenancy, swap points, auth, billing, theme, locales, admin. Anything true of *one* model,
screen or job lives in that thing's own file.

<% if layout == :domains -%>
Generated with `--domains`: each domain nests the same `app/` tree one level in, at
`domains/<name>/app/models/…`, and declares `depends_on`, `exposes` and `publishes_events` in
`domains/<name>/domain.rb`. Cross-domain direct model access fails at boot.
<% else -%>
This app is **flat** — everything in `app/<kind>/`, no `domains/` directory. That is right until
more than one team owns parts of `app/`, or `app/models/` passes ~25 models. The move to domains is
`git mv` plus one `domain.rb`: a path-prefix change, and nothing inside a moved file changes.
<% end -%>

## Non-negotiables that apply to your app code

Enforced at boot, not by review. Breaking one is a `MAGIK_*` failure with a runnable `fix:`.

| Rule | What it means here |
|---|---|
| **Money is integer cents** | the `:money` field type. **No `Float` near currency** — not in a model, a test, a fixture or a seed |
| **A ledger is the only thing that moves money** | a `balance` column an action increments is the bug double-entry exists to prevent. Debits must equal credits or boot fails |
| **Multi-tenant by default** | every model auto-scoped by `tenant_id`. A query with no `tenant_id` predicate is a `magik check --scale` finding |
| **UUIDv7 primary keys** | from the first migration. Sortable, shard-safe, no sequence to reconcile after a split |
| **An action is the only thing that mutates** | `ls app/actions/` is meant to be the complete list of writes in the product |
| **A screen never queries the database** | it names a `state`; the state calls a scope the model owns. A `Sequel` dataset built inside a `body` block is refused |
| **A job is the only thing that runs async** | a fiber or thread spawned outside a `job` is untracked and unretryable |
| **Stateless app servers** | no in-process session or UI state across requests. An `@ivar` on a screen that survives a request is refused |
| **No SPA framework** | server-rendered HTML plus htmx, compiled from the DSL. Never add React, Vue, a bundler or a client build step |
| **No offline, no heavy client compute** | the server is the single source of truth. A canvas editor or a game loop is out of scope — say so and refuse rather than half-building it |
| **SOLID, SRP hardest** | one class, one reason to change. New behaviour is a new declaration in its own file, never another branch in a growing `case` |
| **Minitest, never RSpec** | tests live in `test/` as `*_test.rb`. There is no `spec/` |
| **No secrets in the tree** | `.env` and `config/master.key` are gitignored and stay that way; `.env.example` holds names and blanks |
| **Lint is not yours to configure** | `.rubocop.yml` `inherit_gem`s the rules from the magik gem. Override a cop in your own file with a reason; never copy the rules in |

## Commands

| Task | Command |
|---|---|
| the dev server | `magik server` |
| tests | `magik test` · one file: `magik test test/models/invoice_test.rb` · `magik test --changed` |
| **the gate** | `bin/check` — lint, tests, `magik check`, and every rule in `scripts/checks/`. `--json`, `--only <step>`, `--list` |
| just the guardrails | `magik check` — every boot rule at once. `--json` for findings as data |
| sharding pain, pre-empted | `magik check --scale` |
| scaffold a declaration and its test | `magik generate model\|screen\|component\|action\|job\|channel\|ledger\|migration\|domain <Name>` |
| the database | `magik db create` · `migrate` · `rollback --step 1` · `seed` · `schema` |
| a REPL with the app loaded | `magik console` · `--sandbox` rolls the session back on exit |
| what a route or a code means | `magik routes` · `magik errors explain MAGIK_LEDGER_UNBALANCED` |
| the domain graph | `magik domains --json` |
| refresh this file's framework half | `magik generate agents --update` |

Every command takes `--json`. Exit codes are contract: `0` success · `1` your app is wrong ·
`2` the invocation is wrong.

## The loop

1. **Name the screen or action the change is for.** A change with no user-visible reason has no test.
2. **`magik generate` rather than hand-placing a file.** The generator knows the layout; a path you
   invented is a `MAGIK_DECLARATION_MISPLACED` waiting to happen.
3. **Make the generated test fail before you make it pass.**
4. **`magik check`, then `magik test`** — in that order. A guardrail failure explains a test failure,
   never the reverse.
5. **Tick `docs/PLAN.md`.** A plan that outlives its facts is worse than no plan.

**Run `bin/check` before claiming anything is done.** Nothing else counts. It exits `0` green, `1`
this app is wrong, `69` a step is missing or not implemented yet — and `69` is not a pass. If a
magik step is still `planned`, say so plainly rather than reporting a green run you did not get.

**Noticed a convention? Write it as a check.** A rule in this file is advice an agent may not follow
on feature #40; the same rule in `scripts/checks/` is a gate failure with a runnable `fix:`, forever.
That is the whole argument for boot-time guardrails, applied to *your* rules — `scripts/README.md`
has the how, and it is a short file.

